  <section class="sp-section sp-history">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">A little history</span>
      <h2 class="disp">The one time we finished the job.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sp-history-grid">
      <article>
        <h3 class="disp">An old defense, badly aimed.</h3>
        <p>Centuries before Jenner, healers across China, India, and the Ottoman Empire practiced <em>variolation</em> — deliberately infecting a healthy person with matter from a mild smallpox case to trigger immunity. It worked, most of the time. It also killed roughly 1–2% of the people it was meant to protect, and could still spread the disease to others. Lady Mary Wortley Montagu, who had survived smallpox and lost a brother to it, brought the practice to England in 1721.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3 class="disp">Then a milkmaid's disease pointed the way out.</h3>
        <p>In 1796, Edward Jenner noticed that milkmaids who'd caught cowpox — a mild relative of smallpox — never seemed to catch the real thing. He deliberately infected an eight-year-old boy with cowpox, then exposed him to smallpox itself. The boy stayed healthy. It was reckless by any modern standard, and it was also the first vaccine ever made against any disease — <em>vaccina</em>, Latin for "of the cow," gave the practice its name.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="sp-timeline-outer">
      <button type="button" class="tl-arrow prev" id="tlPrev" aria-label="Previous milestone">‹</button>
      <div class="sp-timeline" id="spTimeline" aria-label="Smallpox milestones" tabindex="0">
        <div class="tl-sp" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">Antiquity</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">The pharaoh's scars</b><p>Pustular lesions consistent with smallpox are found on the mummified skin of Ramesses V, who died around 1145 BCE — the earliest physical evidence of the disease.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">1000s CE</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Variolation begins</b><p>Physicians in China and India are already deliberately exposing patients to matter from mild cases, trading a small, controlled risk for immunity.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">1721</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Lady Montagu's import</b><p>Having survived smallpox and lost her brother to it, Lady Mary Wortley Montagu brings variolation from the Ottoman Empire to England.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev ok"><div class="yr">1796</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Jenner's cowpox gambit</b><p>Edward Jenner tests his cowpox theory on eight-year-old James Phipps, creating the first vaccine against any disease.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">1853</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Made compulsory</b><p>Britain's Vaccination Act mandates smallpox vaccination for infants — one of the first national vaccine mandates anywhere.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">1950s</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Gone from the rich world</b><p>Routine vaccination clears smallpox from North America and most of Europe, even as it still kills millions a year elsewhere.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev sev"><div class="yr">1959</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">A resolution with no teeth</b><p>The World Health Assembly calls for global eradication, but funding and commitment are thin. The disease barely notices.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev ok"><div class="yr">1966</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Foege's ring</b><p>In Nigeria, CDC epidemiologist William Foege runs short of vaccine and abandons mass campaigns for surveillance-and-containment instead — ring vaccination works better than the original plan ever did.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">1967</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">The Intensified Eradication Programme</b><p>WHO relaunches the campaign with real funding, D.A. Henderson leading a coordinated global push, and ring vaccination as doctrine.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">1971</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Gone from South America</b><p>Brazil records the last case anywhere in the Americas.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">1975</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Gone from Asia</b><p>Rahima Banu, age two, in Bangladesh, becomes the last person infected with <em>Variola major</em> — the deadlier of the two smallpox strains — anywhere on Earth.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev ok"><div class="yr">1977</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">The last natural case</b><p>Ali Maow Maalin, a hospital cook in Merca, Somalia, becomes the last person ever infected with smallpox outside a laboratory.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev sev"><div class="yr">1978</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">The last death</b><p>Janet Parker dies in Birmingham after a laboratory containment failure — the disease's final victim, a year after its natural extinction.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev ok"><div class="yr">1980</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Certified gone</b><p>The World Health Assembly formally certifies the global eradication of smallpox — the only time humanity has erased a human disease from the wild.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">2022</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">The gap it left</b><p>Mpox, a viral cousin, spreads globally for the first time — moving into some of the immunity gap left by decades without routine smallpox vaccination.</p></div></div>
