  <section class="sp-section sp-ring-section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">The cool diagram</span>
      <h2 class="disp">The ring that beat a pandemic.</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="sp-sub">Every other diagram on this site shows something declining. This one climbs: instead of racing to vaccinate everyone, teams found each case, then vaccinated only the ring of people around it — fast enough to break the chain before it spread further. It beat mass vaccination to zero using a fraction of the doses. Hover a step to read it.</p>
    <div class="sp-ring" id="spRing"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="sp-section sp-spotlight">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">When "eradicated" wasn't quite true · Birmingham, 1978</span>
      <h2 class="disp">The last person smallpox ever killed.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sp-spotlight-grid">
      <article class="sp-spotlight-lead">
        <p>By August 1978, the world's last natural case was already a year in the past. Janet Parker, a medical photographer at Birmingham Medical School, worked one floor above a research laboratory that still studied live smallpox virus. Investigators traced her infection to a fault in a shared service duct that let virus drift upward from the lab into her workspace — a decade of fieldwork undone by a few feet of ductwork.</p>
        <p>She died on September 11, 1978, eleven months after the disease's last natural case. Her mother caught it from her and survived; the lab's director, Professor Henry Bedson, died by suicide during the investigation that followed. The lab was closed for good, and the case forced a permanent rewrite of the rules: after 1978, live smallpox virus would legally exist in only two places on Earth.</p>
      </article>
      <aside class="sp-spotlight-facts">
        <div class="sp-fact"><span class="n">1978</span><span class="k">the year smallpox killed for the last time — a year <em>after</em> eradication, not before</span></div>
        <div class="sp-fact"><span class="n">1</span><span class="k">shared ventilation duct — the containment failure that undid it</span></div>
        <div class="sp-fact"><span class="n">2</span><span class="k">labs on Earth still legally permitted to hold the live virus, CDC (USA) and VECTOR (Russia)</span></div>
        <div class="sp-fact sp-fact-hope"><span class="n">0</span><span class="k">confirmed human smallpox deaths since 1978 — the record still holds</span></div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sp-section sp-close">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">The reckoning</span>
      <h2 class="disp">Beaten once. Never fully gone.</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="sp-close-line">Smallpox is the only disease human beings have ever erased from the wild — proof that a funded, coordinated, unglamorous public-health campaign can finish what it starts. But it's also the only disease that could come back by <b>decision</b> rather than chance: two declared stockpiles still exist, a 2017 study proved a related virus could be built from mail-order DNA, and routine vaccination ended decades ago, leaving most of the world with no immunity to the family of viruses it belongs to — a gap mpox is already finding its way into. We know exactly how to win this fight. Whether we do it again is a choice, not a rerun.</p>
